---
// Part 1 — copy from the 5 Nov Linear tickets (ADA-45–48), one 70ch column. Each of
// the doc's section breaks opens on a full-bleed <Scene>; the photographs are
// from Unsplash (credits in src/assets/story/README.md).
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import ReadProgress from '../components/ReadProgress.astro';
import fire from '../assets/story/problem-v2-01-fire.jpg';
import westminster from '../assets/story/problem-v2-02-westminster-dusk.jpg';
import twoSides from '../assets/story/problem-v2-03-two-sides.jpg';
import unionFlags from '../assets/story/problem-v2-04-union-flags.jpg';
---

<Layout
  title="The Real Problem — We The People"
  introLines={['The Real', 'Problem']}
  joinCollapsed
>
  <PageHero lines={['The Real', 'Problem']} />

  <ReadProgress />
  <article class="prose">
    <Scene
      first
      eager
      src={fire}
      alt="Smoke and flames pouring from the roof of a block of flats"
      heading="We all know things are going wrong. We can all feel it."
    />
    <p>
      Sometimes it feels like there are so many ways we’re facing disaster for ourselves, our
      children, our future, everything that matters to us.
    </p>
    <p>
      Like you, everyone has their own list: cost of living, immigration, the housing crisis,
      war, the climate, even the basics of life, like having enough food on the table or the
      poisoning of our water and air.
    </p>
    <p>
      Even in those moments when politicians do take action, it never feels like enough.
      We're left feeling let down, angry, afraid and like we have no power. Meanwhile the
      pressure on us and our country just keeps mounting.
    </p>
    <p>We <em>can</em> turn it around.</p>
    <p>But only if we go to the heart of the mess.</p>

    <Scene
      src={westminster}
      alt="The Houses of Parliament in silhouette against a stormy evening sky"
      heading="The system is broken"
    />
    <p>The simple fact is that our system does not work for us any more.</p>
    <p>
      Just 10% of people have high confidence in the UK Government. 84% say the system needs
      to change in some way. (<a
        href="https://www.electoralcommission.org.uk/research-reports-and-data/public-attitudes/public-attitudes-elections-and-democracy-2026-findings"
        rel="noopener"
        target="_blank">Electoral Commission, 2026</a
      >)
    </p>
    <p>To know why, just look at our politicians.</p>
    <p>
      They’re supposed to be our voice within the system. But it’s not clear if any of them
      put our interests first. Perhaps red-tape, finance and the global system make it near
      impossible.
    </p>
    <p>Still, only 9% of us think they are generally trustworthy.</p>
    <p>
      When things go wrong, the promise of our system is that we can elect someone different,
      someone better. But how often do we vote with high hopes, only for nothing to change?
    </p>
    <p>No wonder more than half of us (52%) feel elections barely reflect what we want, if at all.</p>
    <p>Clearly, we need profound change – the transformation of our system.</p>
    <p>
      But that’s not going to happen until we face an even deeper problem. Perhaps the most
      dangerous of all.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={twoSides}
      alt="Two protests split down the middle: Stand Up To Racism placards on one side, St George's flags on the other"
      heading="We feel deeply divided"
    />
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://www.kcl.ac.uk/news/uks-sense-of-division-reaches-new-high-as-culture-war-tensions-grow-study-finds"
        rel="noopener"
        target="_blank">84% of people</a
      > say this country feels divided. It’s not just politics. We feel it in our lives.
    </p>
    <p>
      The relative we can’t talk to without arguing. The old friend you’ve neglected as their
      views have become more extreme. The fear of saying the wrong thing to the wrong group and
      being shamed for it. All of that before we even go online.
    </p>
    <p>
      It’s easy to see why it’s getting worse. Our daily lives are getting tougher. Tech has
      made us lonelier and more disconnected. And our media, old and new, profits by pitting us
      against one another.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our politicians don’t help. They just pitch to one side or the other in their bid for
      power.
    </p>
    <p>It’s much harder to see the way out.</p>
    <p>
      This is becoming a political emergency. The kind that has led to serious violence on the
      streets or harsh government measures to maintain order, in other places, at other times.
    </p>
    <p>
      We've lost our common ground, both a sense of shared values and a commitment to
      understanding, just when we need it most.
    </p>

    <Scene
      src={unionFlags}
      alt="A crowd waving Union flags, arms raised and cheering"
      heading="But We The People still believe in democracy"
    />
    <p>
      Our future depends on us rediscovering our ability to talk, listen and reach the middle
      ground, no matter how difficult it seems right now.
    </p>
    <p>
      As long as we're divided and incoherent, politicians can choose who to listen to and who
      to ignore, playing us against each other. We can’t push for change while we're fighting
      amongst ourselves.
    </p>
    <p>
      But if we come back together, we’ll be too strong to dismiss. From there, we can turn
      anger into answers and find a way forward that works for us again.
    </p>
    <p>We can reunite the country. We can transform the system.</p>
    <p>Of course, politicians, parties and elections won’t do this for us.</p>
    <p>We need something new.</p>

    <p class="pull">That’s where we come in.</p>

    <a class="back" href="/hope">Our Real Hope →</a>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
    var back = document.querySelector('article.prose .back');
    if (!back) return;
    var fired = false;
    var observer = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      entries.forEach(function (entry) {
        if (entry.isIntersecting && !fired) {
          fired = true;
          window.posthog?.capture('problem_article_read');
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      });
    }, { threshold: 0.5 });
    observer.observe(back);
  });
</script>
